      "Language": "ita",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "One-hundred consecutive procedures of elective coronary angioplasty were attempted in 95 patients, in the absence of any surgical stand-by. All patients had angina and/or signs of inducible ischemia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "All patients and attending physicians were informed that no surgical coverage was available, and gave their consent. The choice was made on the basis of the estimate, on clinical and angiographic grounds, of the consequences of vessel occlusion, of the possible help offered by percutaneous bail-out techniques, and of the applicability of surgical stand-by."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A primary success was achieved in 92 cases: in 5 cases a Palmaz-Schatz stent was implanted. In 6 cases the procedure was unsuccessful, with no complications. Two patients sustained a myocardial infarction without new Q-waves. No patient died, nor underwent cardiac surgery within 1 month of discharge."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our initial experience suggests that, in the absence of surgical stand-by, elective coronary angioplasty can be performed in selected patients at an acceptable risk."
        }
      ]},
